Built with [Hardhat](https://hardhat.org) and [Fhenix CoFHE](https://cofhesdk.fhenix.io) — encrypted
state lives on-chain as ciphertext handles and all arithmetic runs on the CoFHE coprocessor.

## Layout

- `contracts/{{contractName}}.sol` — the {{patternLabel}} contract
- `test/{{contractName}}.test.ts` — mock-network tests
- `scripts/deploy.ts` — deployment script
{{#if frontend}}- `frontend/` — {{frontendLabel}} dApp
{{/if}}

## Getting started

```bash
cp .env.example .env
{{pmRun}} compile
{{pmRun}} test
```

Tests run against the CoFHE mock contracts, which `@cofhe/hardhat-plugin` deploys into the Hardhat
network automatically. No testnet or API key is needed, and `PRIVATE_KEY` may stay unset.

To deploy locally, start a node — which also deploys the mocks — and point the script at it:

```bash
{{pmRun}} hardhat node
{{pmRun}} hardhat run scripts/deploy.ts --network localhost
```

`cofhe deploy` does the same thing without editing the script, and records the address:

```bash
npx cofhe deploy {{contractName}} --network localhost
```
{{#if frontend}}
## Frontend

```bash
{{pmRun}} dev
```

Set `{{frontendEnvPrefix}}CONTRACT_ADDRESS` in `frontend/{{frontendEnvFile}}` after deploying.

The page encrypts in the browser, so the CoFHE client is loaded client-side only and the whole
frontend is served with `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy`/`Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` set — that is
what lets tfhe prove across several wasm threads instead of one.
{{/if}}
{{#if frontend}}
## Hosting the frontend

The page proves in the browser, so the host has to send two headers for tfhe to use a
multi-threaded wasm pool. They are an optimisation, not a requirement: without them the SDK proves
on a single thread instead of failing, which its own benchmark puts at roughly 5x slower.

The page itself reports which mode it got, and the SDK logs it too — look for
`[Worker] TFHE initialized` in the browser console to see the thread count or the reason it fell
back.

```
Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
```

{{frontendHostingNote}}

Point the host at `frontend/` as the project root, set `{{frontendEnvPrefix}}CONTRACT_ADDRESS` in its
environment, and deploy.
{{/if}}
## Deploying to Arbitrum Sepolia

Arbitrum Sepolia is CoFHE's primary testnet.

1. Put a funded key in `.env` as `PRIVATE_KEY`.
2. Deploy:

   ```bash
   {{pmRun}} deploy:arb
   ```

Ethereum Sepolia is also supported via `{{pmRun}} deploy:sepolia`.

`npx cofhe deploy {{contractName}} --network arb-sepolia` is the same deployment with the address
recorded in `deployments.json`{{#if frontend}} and written to `{{frontendEnvPrefix}}CONTRACT_ADDRESS`
in `frontend/{{frontendEnvFile}}`{{/if}}. It also takes any other contract in `contracts/`, which the
scripts above do not.

### Testnet funds

- Arbitrum Sepolia ETH: https://faucet.quicknode.com/arbitrum/sepolia
- Ethereum Sepolia ETH: https://faucet.quicknode.com/ethereum/sepolia
- CoFHE faucet task: `npx hardhat task:cofhe:use-faucet --network arb-sepolia`

## Adding another pattern

```bash
npx cofhe add <pattern>
```

Drops a second pattern's contract and test next to this one — `sealed-bid`, `private-vote`,
`encrypted-token`, `encrypted-escrow` or `blank`. Nothing already here is overwritten. Run
`npx cofhe add` with no argument for the list.

## Working with encrypted values

Encrypted inputs are produced off-chain and verified on-chain as a batch:

```ts
const [handle, signature] = await client
  .encryptInputs([Encryptable.uint32(42n)])
  .setConsumingContract(await contract.getAddress())
  .execute()
```

The contract converts them with `FHE.asEuint32s(handles, signature)` and must call `FHE.allowThis`
on anything it stores, plus `FHE.allowSender` or `FHE.allow(handle, account)` for whoever is allowed
to decrypt it.

When a decrypt fails, the handle itself tells you nothing. Inspect it instead:

```bash
npx cofhe handle <ctHash> --network arb-sepolia
```

It decodes the type and reports who is allowed to decrypt it.

